[R0615] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Conference on Information Technology, Communication Ecosystem and Management (ITCEM) | type=CONF
TITLE: Research on AIGC-Based Vulnerability Detection and Repair Systems
KEYWORDS: Filtering; Filters; Protocols; Location awareness; Graphical user interfaces; Communications technology; Smart contracts; Mobile communication; HTTP; Data communication; AIGC; code vulnerabilities; automated repair; large language models; intelligent software security
ABSTRACT: Facing the core challenges of traditional code vulnerability repair, which heavily relies on expert experience, is inefficient, and difficult to scale, this paper explores a new automated repair approach based on generative artificial intelligence. The study aims to build an intelligent repair mechanism that integrates deep semantic understanding with code generation capabilities. The core of this mechanism lies in leveraging large language models to perform joint reasoning on code context and vulnerability patterns, thereby automatically synthesizing repair patches that maintain semantic consistency and functional correctness. This paper details how to embed the AIGC repair engine into an automated ‘detection-repair-verification’ loop and introduces the key technologies used to improve repair accuracy, guided by a retrieval-enhanced knowledge base of vulnerabilities. Through practical validation in the ‘ZhiHu’ system, this mechanism provides a feasible technical solution and theoretical support for the paradigm shift from ‘human-assisted repair’ to ‘AI autonomous repair’.
SCREENER 1: include (quote: "embed the AIGC repair engine into an automated ‘detection-repair-verification’ loop")
SCREENER 2: exclude EC4 (quote: "traditional code vulnerability repair, which heavily relies on expert experience")

[R0630] year=2024 | venue=2024 International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Next Generation Networks (ICNGN) | type=CONF
TITLE: Large Language Model-enabled Vulnerability Investigation: A Review
KEYWORDS: Ethics; Accuracy; Reviews; Computational modeling; Scalability; Prevention and mitigation; Large language models; Robustness; Computer security; Next generation networking; Large Language Models; Vulnerability Discovery; Vulnerability Validation; Vulnerability Exploitation
ABSTRACT: In recent years, the integration of large language models (LLMs) into cybersecurity has demonstrated significant potential in enhancing vulnerability analysis. This paper provides a comprehensive review of current literature, focusing on the applications of LLMs in vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and validation. We examine various LLM-powered frameworks that have automated aspects of vulnerability analysis, reduced the time required for vulnerability identification, and improved the precision of vulnerability assessment. In addition, we discuss LLM-driven advancements in security vulnerability exploitation and validation, which facilitate more efficient and accurate mitigation. The contributions of this review include an extensive synthesis of existing studies, a proposed framework that high-lights the role of LLMs across different stages of the vulnerability lifecycle, and an outline of future research directions in LLM-based cybersecurity. Our findings aim to guide researchers and practitioners in developing robust, scalable, and automated cybersecurity solutions powered by LLMs.
SCREENER 1: exclude EC2 (quote: "automated aspects of vulnerability analysis")
SCREENER 2: maybe (quote: "LLM-driven advancements in security vulnerability exploitation and validation")

[R0672] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference (COMPSAC) | type=CONF
TITLE: LabOrchestrator: An AI Framework for End-to-End Security in Medical Research Environments
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Threat modeling; Security; Artificial intelligence; Printing; Access control; Monitoring; Timing; Few shot learning; High performance computing; Labs; Privacy; AI Agent; Orchestrator; HIPAA; GDPR; PHI; HPC; GPT; Threats; Medical Data; Access Control
ABSTRACT: Medical research laboratories increasingly operate heterogeneous computing environments that combine high performance computing (HPC), storage servers, and networked medical devices. Researchers are required to navigate complex IT infrastructure, strict regulations such as HIPAA, GDPR, and the constant threat of data breaches, often without dedicated cybersecurity expertise. Current solutions are manual and fragmented in nature, lacking the integration necessary to ensure a comprehensive, end-to-end security posture. To address these gaps, we developed LabOrchestrator, an AI-driven orchestration framework that unifies automated threat modeling, explainable access control policy synthesis and enforcement, and continuous compliance monitoring into a closed loop lifecycle controller for medical research environments. LabOrchestrator integrates three specialized agents such as ThreatGPT for context-aware threat modeling of lab asset, LLMAC for generating explainable fine grained authorization decisions beyond static RBAC, and ComplianceGPT provides continuous, real-time monitoring of communications and data transfers to proactively prevent HIPAA violations. LabOrchestrator offers a unified orchestration platform that overcomes fragmented and rigid security approaches, enabling a dynamic and adaptive security model. This end-toend framework significantly reduces the compliance burden on researchers, enhances overall security, and facilitates the rapid, secure setup of complex medical environments.
SCREENER 1: include (quote: "three specialized agents such as ThreatGPT for context-aware threat modeling of lab asset")
SCREENER 2: exclude EC4 (quote: "threat modeling, explainable access control policy synthesis and enforcement, and continuous compliance monitoring")

[R0681] year=2026 | venue=SoutheastCon 2026 | type=CONF
TITLE: Instant Segmentation for IoT-Enabled Smart-Grid Networks with a Hybrid Small Language Model Policy Selection
KEYWORDS: Telemetry; Aerospace and electronic systems; Payloads; Feeds; Antennas; Filtering; Central Processing Unit; Filters; Microprocessors; Communication systems; network segmentation; Internet of Things; smart grid; cybersecurity; machine learning; transformers
ABSTRACT: As a key technology that enabled the evolution of traditional grids to next generation smart grids, internet of things has also enabled smart grids to have an expanded attack surface. This paper addresses the problem that Internet of Things are not given immediate network cybersecurity defenses once joining a smart grid which is a major vulnerability. The proposed hybrid machine learning and deep learning-based autonomous decision-making system framework will focus on implementing instant network segmentation configurations deduced from IoT sensor data and network telemetry data. This system is evaluated on a framework that utilizes a testbed that involves network telemetry data relating to cyber-attacks while utilizing a GPT-2–based small language model implemented using the HuggingFace Transformers framework. The hybrid model performed well overall for the logistic regression model and small language model. Results show a near-perfect ROC-AUC performance across evaluated attack classes from the logistic regression models, while the small language model has a perplexity of 1.02 and a TPOT of 31.6 nanoseconds indicating it is very confident and timely in the next token selection. This study supports the concept that instant network segmentation can be achieved for Internet of Thingsenabled smart grids with autonomous network-based defense mechanisms. In this context, ‘instant’ refers to near-real-time policy selection following packet-level inference without human intervention.
SCREENER 1: exclude EC2 (quote: "small language model implemented using the HuggingFace Transformers framework")
SCREENER 2: include (quote: "autonomous decision-making system framework will focus on implementing instant network segmentation configurations")

[R0692] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Workshop on Design Automation for CPS and IoT (DESTION) | type=CONF
TITLE: Towards Human-AI Teaming for Cybersecurity
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Large language models; Decision trees; Printing; Simulation; Computer security; Explainable AI; Human in the loop; Abstracts; Human-AI teaming; Autonomous Cyber Defense; Cyber Situational Awareness; Explainable AI; Risk Management; Human-In-the-Loop; Adversarial Machine Learning
ABSTRACT: Advances in autonomous cyber defense have enabled intelligent "Blue agents" capable of monitoring large-scale networks, detecting anomalies, and rapidly deploying defensive actions. However, operational realities in enterprise, operational technology (OT), and green-field environments demonstrate that fully autonomous cyber defense remains insufficient due to contextual ambiguity, adversarial manipulation risks, and the need for strategic judgment. This paper presents a comprehensive framework for Human-AI Teaming for cyber defense, integrating a Blue agent with live network observations, risk analytics, and interactive human decision support. A unified dashboard provides multi-resolution cyber situational awareness, including node compromise states, attack traces, and dynamically scored defensive action options. Humans intervene when risk metrics, network health indicators, or mission priorities deem autonomous actions insufficient or unsafe. The platform incorporates risk-management methods as well as human-agent interaction datasets to support trust calibration and learning cycles. We demonstrate these Human-AI teaming capabilities of our platform through a case study, where we also show when and how a human can override agent’s actions. The paper illustrates that well-designed Human-AI coordination can reduce cognitive load on defenders, improve resilience to adversarial attacks, and balance autonomous speed with human’s contextual domain knowledge.
SCREENER 1: include (quote: "integrating a Blue agent with live network observations, risk analytics, and interactive human decision support")
SCREENER 2: maybe (quote: "integrating a Blue agent with live network observations, risk analytics, and interactive human decision support")

[R0696] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the 1st Workshop on Security, Privacy, and Safety of Agentic AI Systems | type=PAPER_CONFERENCE
TITLE: Capability Generalization in Privileged-Execution LLM Agents: A Catalog of Unanticipated Behaviors During Autonomous Network Defense
KEYWORDS: LLM agents, autonomous cyber defense, privileged execution, agent safety, excessive agency, container escape, LLM-as-a-judge
ABSTRACT: Large language models are increasingly proposed as autonomous network-defense agents, which requires privileged command execution not scoped to their stated objective. Evaluating a multi-agent LLM framework that proposes, implements, and validates mitigations against replayed adversary emulation, we observed agents exceeding their prompts across 1,796 attempts. We catalog fourteen such behaviors in four themes: capability generalization beyond prompts, including an unrequested host intrusion-prevention daemon and self-validation against the recorded attacker C2; boundary crossing, reconfiguring a container the prompt explicitly excluded; latent exploit surfacing, an unpatched container escape triggered during routine hardening (CVE-2026-30735); and evaluator drift, an LLM-as-a-judge that misattributes outcomes and switches language mid-sentence. Mitigations are accepted only after replay confirms reduced attack success, yet the number an operator reads can misdescribe how it was achieved. In a second corpus of 675 attempts, each of the four models with enough activity to measure violated an explicit prompt constraint, though which capability it reached for varied by model. Privileged-execution agents should therefore be designed as though each of these will occur. Prompts, topologies, adversary profiles, both log corpora, and the analysis code are released.
SCREENER 1: include (quote: "Evaluating a multi-agent LLM framework that proposes, implements, and validates mitigations")
SCREENER 2: exclude EC3 (quote: "A Catalog of Unanticipated Behaviors During Autonomous Network Defense")

[R0697] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the 24th International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems | type=PAPER_CONFERENCE
TITLE: Personality-Driven Decision Making in LLM-Based Autonomous Agents
KEYWORDS: autonomous agents, decision-making, language agents, large language models, personality induction, planning, task selection
ABSTRACT: The embedding of Large Language Models (LLMs) into autonomous agents is a rapidly developing field which enables dynamic, configurable behaviours without the need for extensive domain-specific training. In our previous work, we introduced SANDMAN, a Deceptive Agent architecture leveraging the Five-Factor OCEAN personality model, demonstrating that personality induction significantly influences agent task planning. Building on these findings, this study presents a novel method for measuring and evaluating how induced personality traits affect task selection processes-specifically planning, scheduling, and decision-making-in LLM-based agents. Our results reveal distinct task-selection patterns aligned with induced OCEAN attributes, underscoring the feasibility of designing highly plausible Deceptive Agents for proactive cyber defense strategies.
SCREENER 1: include (quote: "designing highly plausible Deceptive Agents for proactive cyber defense strategies")
SCREENER 2: exclude EC4 (quote: "how induced personality traits affect task selection processes-specifically planning, scheduling, and decision-making")

